ANSWERS:

Part 1 - Theoretical Questions:

1)
    a) First of all, sigsetjmp() and siglongjmp() functions are used to implement non-local
    jumps in signal handlers,
    they are useful for handling errors or exceptions in the programs. sigsetjmp() sets a jump point
    in the program that can be later used by siglongjmp() to return to that point of the program,
    in other words sigsetjmp() saves the stack environment and siglongjmp() restores it.

    b) The sigsetjmp() and siglongjmp() functions can be used to temporarily mask signals and prevent
    them from interrupting a critical section of code, the sigsetjmp() function saves the signal mask
    at the calling time, while the siglongjmp() function restores the saved signal mask after
    returning from a signal handler.

2) User-level threads are useful in implementing parallelism in applications, 
by user-level threads we can divide the work of an application into smaller tasks.
For example, a data processing application can use user-level threads to concurrently process
different parts of a large data set, due to the fact that user-level threads are managed 
entirely in user space the overhead of context switching and synchronisation is reduced.

3) advantages:
    1) Security - By creating a new process for each tab and allocating private
    memory space for each process, Chrome is able to isolate each tab from the others.

    2) Stability - By isolating each tab in its own process, Chrome is able to 
    improve stability, for example if a tab crashes it does not affect the other tabs.

    3) Simplified debugging: When each tab running in its own process, it becomes 
    easier to debug and troubleshoot issues, as each process can be analysed separately.

   disadvantages:
    1) Resource usage - Creating a new process for each tab requires more system 
    resources than creating a kernel-level thread for example: larger memory space,
    system resources overhead etc.

    2) Overall complexity: Managing multiple processes can be more complex than
    managing multiple threads, as each process has its own set of system resources and
    must be managed separately.

    3) Disk usage: Each process requires its own cache and temporary files, which
    may increase the amount of disk space used by the browser.

4) we've opened the "Shotwell" application on the CS computer:
    a) we entered "ps -A" in the shell, and returned 177178 as the Shotwell pid.
    b) we entered "kill pid" in shell, the application Shotwell closed.
    c) When we type on the Keyboard in shell, an interrupt is sent from the keyboard hardware
    to the CPU. The interrupt is handled by an OS function in the interrupt vector,
    then the OS sends a Signal to the shell allowing the shell to display the letters
    typed on the Screen. When we want to open the application (Shotwell), we switch to kernel mode and
    The OS allocates memory for a new process (sets up resources for the application).
    When we type "kill pid", kill() system call in the OS is invoked. kill() sends a Signal to the 
    process. The default Signal sent by kill() is SIGTERM, which asks the process 
    (in our case, shotwell application) to terminate.

5) Virtual time - The running time of a process as perceived by that process it can differ from real
time due to factors such as process scheduling, CPU usage etc. Virtual time is important for tasks that 
need to measure or control the spent time on specific operations, such as simulation and testing. An 
example of using virtual time is measuring a thread's life cycle or measuring the amount of time spent 
on different parts of a program's execution as a performance profiling tool.

Real time -  The passed time on the system clock from the start of the process until its completion, 
including any delays that may occur. Real time is important in several time-critical applications where 
delays of even a few seconds can have significant consequences, An example of using real time is in a 
live video or audio stream